00:13:19.000 --> 00:14:08.000
Normally, that would be a weakness, but here it becomes a strength. Where normally the reader feels that a book is for them, here we feel that we are eavesdropping through a keyhole or from a phone line downstairs. The effect is superficially frustrating, but it also gives Lament a subtle quality to its spirituality that the Red Book lacks. Many of the obvious elements for discussion that one would assume would be in a conversation about the enormous Red Book are completely ignored in the dialogue. Hellman and Shamdasani's main takeaway is that the Red Book is about the dead. What they mean by the dead is never explained directly, and this was a major sticking point for other reviewers, but I think that their point works better undefined. They talk about the dead as a numinous term.
通常，这会是一个弱点，但在这里它变成了一个优点。通常读者会觉得一本书是为他们写的，而在这里，我们感觉自己是在通过钥匙孔或楼下的电话线偷听。这种效果在表面上令人沮丧，但它也赋予了《哀歌》一种《红书》所缺乏的微妙灵性。人们认为在关于宏大的《红书》的对话中应该讨论的许多明显元素，在对话中完全被忽略了。希尔曼和沙姆达萨尼的主要结论是，《红书》是关于“死者”的。他们所说的“死者”从未被直接解释，这是其他评论家的一大症结，但我认为他们的观点在未定义的情况下效果更好。他们将死者视为一个神圣的术语。

20
00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:40.000
Perhaps they are speaking about the reality of death itself. Perhaps about the dead of history. Perhaps they are describing the impenetrable veil that we all see others enter but never see past ourselves. Maybe the concept contains all of these elements. Hellman, who was 82 at the time of having the conversation in Lament, may have been using the Red Book and his dialogue with Shamdasani to come to terms with his feelings about his own impending death. Perhaps it is undefined because these men are feeling something, or intuitively seeing
也许他们谈论的是死亡本身的现实。也许是关于历史的死者。也许他们描述的是我们都看着别人进入，但自己却从未看透的不可逾越的面纱。也许这个概念包含了所有这些元素。希尔曼在进行《哀歌》对话时已经82岁了，他可能一直在利用《红书》和他与沙姆达萨尼的对话来与自己对即将到来的死亡的感受达成和解。也许它之所以未被定义，是因为这些人正在感受某种东西，或者直觉地看到了

21
00:14:40.000 --> 00:15:23.000
something that the living lack the intellectual language for. It is not that the authors do not know what they are talking about, they know, but they are not completely able to say it. Hellman was such an infuriatingly intuitive person and writer that his biggest downfall in his other books is that he often felt truths that he could not articulate, and instead he would retreat into arguing the merits of his credentials and background, or into intellectual archival of his opinions on philosophers and artists and history. And in his other works, this led to a didactic and self-righteous tone that his writing is largely worse for. In Lament, Hillman is forced to talk off the cuff, and that limitation puts
某种活人缺乏智力语言来表达的东西。并不是作者不知道他们在谈论什么，他们知道，但他们无法完全说出来。希尔曼是一位令人恼火的直觉型人物和作家，他在其他著作中最大的失败在于，他经常感受到无法表达的真理，于是他会退缩到争论自己的资历和背景的优点，或者退缩到对他对哲学家、艺术家和历史的观点的知识性归档中。在他的其他作品中，这导致了一种说教和自以为是的语调，使他的写作大打折扣。在《哀歌》中，希尔曼被迫即兴发言，这种局限性使

22
00:15:23.000 --> 00:16:05.000
him at his best as a thinker. In his review of Lament, David Tacey has made the very good point that Jung abandoned the direction that the Red Book was taking him in. Jung saw it as a dead end for the experiential psychology and retreated back into analytical inventorying of archetypes. On the publication of the Red Book, many Jungians celebrated the book as the culmination of Jungian thought, when instead, as Tacey points out, it was merely part of its origins. The book represents a proto-Jungian psychology, as Jung attempted to discover techniques for integration while he was directly experiencing the things that he was directly experiencing when he that he was directly experiencing when
他作为思想家达到了最佳状态。大卫·泰西在对《哀歌》的评论中提出了一个非常好的观点，即荣格放弃了《红书》所引领的方向。荣格将其视为体验心理学的死胡同，并退回到了对原型的分析性盘点中。在《红书》出版时，许多荣格派人士将其庆祝为荣格思想的巅峰，而正如泰西所指出的，它实际上只是其起源的一部分。这本书代表了一种原始的荣格心理学，因为荣格在撰写《红书》时，正试图在直接体验这些事物的同时，寻找整合的技术。

23
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:40.000
he wrote the Red Book. Hellman and Shamdasani probed the psychology's origins for hints of its future in lament. Hellman and Shamdasani's thesis is partially a question about ethics and partially a question about cosmology. Are there any universal directions for living and behaving that Jungian psychology compels us towards? That would be ethics. Is there an external worldview that the notoriously phenomenological nature of Jungian psychology might imply? That would be a cosmology. These are the major questions that Hillman and Shambhasani confront and lament.
希尔曼和沙姆达萨尼在《哀歌》中探究了该心理学的起源，以寻找其未来的线索。希尔曼和沙姆达萨尼的论点部分是关于伦理的问题，部分是关于宇宙论的问题。荣格心理学是否迫使我们走向任何普遍的生活和行为方向？那是伦理学。荣格心理学那著名的现象学本质是否暗示了一种外部的世界观？那是宇宙论。这些是希尔曼和沙姆达萨尼在《哀歌》中面对的主要问题。

24
00:16:40.000 --> 00:17:28.000
Their answer is not an answer as much as it is a question for the psychologists of the future. Their conclusion is that the dead of our families, society, and human history foist their unlived lives upon us. It is up to us and our therapists to help deal with the burden of the dead. It is not us that live, but the dead that live through us. And Hillman quotes W.H. Auden several times to serve his point. The quote that he prefers is, we are lived through powers that we pretend to understand. This was definitely true of Hillman if you've read his other work. A major tenant of Jungian psychology is that adult children struggle under the unlived life of the
他们的回答与其说是一个答案，不如说是给未来心理学家提出的一个问题。他们的结论是，我们家庭、社会和人类历史的死者将他们未竟的生活强加于我们。处理死者的负担取决于我们和我们的治疗师。不是我们在生活，而是死者通过我们生活。希尔曼多次引用W.H.奥登的话来支持他的观点。他最喜欢的引文是：“我们被我们假装理解的力量所驱动。”如果你读过希尔曼的其他作品，这对他来说绝对是真实的。荣格心理学的一个主要原则是，成年子女在父母未竟的生活下挣扎。

25
00:17:28.000 --> 00:18:10.000
parent. The Jungian analyst helps the patient acknowledge and integrate all the forces of the psyche that the parent ran from and so they are not passed down to future generations. A passive implication of the ethics and the cosmology laid out in Lament is that to have a future we must reckon with not only the unlived life of the parent, but also the unlived life of the dead, our civilization, our history, and our humanity, and the people who have failed at the projects that we are picking up. It is our job as the living to answer the questions and face the contradictions that our humanity posits in order answer the questions and face the contradictions that our humanity posits in order to discover who and what we really are.
荣格分析师帮助病人承认并整合父母逃避的所有心灵力量，这样它们就不会传给后代。《哀歌》中提出的伦理和宇宙论的一个被动含义是，为了拥有未来，我们不仅必须面对父母未竟的生活，还必须面对死者、我们的文明、我们的历史、我们的人性，以及那些在我们正在接手的项目中失败的人们。作为生者，我们的工作是回答问题并面对我们人性所提出的矛盾，以便发现我们到底是谁、是什么。

26
00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:46.000
The half-truths and outright lies from the past masquerade as tradition for tradition's sake, literalized religion, and unconscious tribal identity, but that must be overthrown. The weight of the debt of history can remain immovable if we try to merely discard it, but it drowns us if we cling to it too tightly. We need to use our history and our tradition to give us a container to reckon with the future. The container must remain flexible if we are to grow into our humanity as a society and an aware people. If you find yourself saying, yes, but what does the dead mean? Then this book is probably not for you.
来自过去的半真半假和彻头彻尾的谎言，伪装成传统、字面化的宗教和无意识的部落认同，但这些必须被推翻。如果我们试图仅仅丢弃历史债务的重量，它可能会纹丝不动，但如果我们抓得太紧，它就会淹没我们。我们需要利用我们的历史和传统来为我们提供一个容器，以应对未来。如果我们作为一个社会和一个有意识的民族要成长为我们的人性，这个容器必须保持灵活。如果你发现自己在说：“是的，但‘死者’是什么意思？”那么这本书可能不适合你。

27
00:18:46.000 --> 00:19:32.000
If you find yourself confused by this review, but humbled by this thesis, then perhaps this book is for you. Instead of a further explanation of the ethical and cosmological future for psychology that this book posits, I'll give you a tangible example about how its message was liberatory for me. Oddly enough, the idea of a descent and return will already be familiar to many Americans through the work of Joseph Campbell. Campbell took the same ethics of descent and return to the unconscious as the model for his monomyth model of storytelling. This briefly influenced psychology and comparative religion in the U.S. and had a major impact on screenwriters to this day. Most of Campbell's influence on psychology is contained to the 1970s and 80s.
如果你对这篇书评感到困惑，但对这个论点感到谦卑，那么这本书可能适合你。与其进一步解释这本书所提出的心理学的伦理和宇宙论未来，我将给你一个具体的例子，说明它的信息对我来说是如何具有解放意义的。奇怪的是，通过约瑟夫·坎贝尔的作品，许多美国人已经熟悉了“下降与回归”的概念。坎贝尔采用了同样的下降到无意识并回归的伦理，作为他叙事“英雄之旅”模型的模型。这在短期内影响了美国的心理学和比较宗教学，并对至今的编剧产生了重大影响。坎贝尔对心理学的大部分影响仅限于20世纪70年代和80年代。

28
00:19:32.000 --> 00:20:27.000
Campbell's ethics are the same as Jung's, though. If one becomes stuck on the monomyth wheel, or the journey to the unconscious and then the return to the integrated conscious, then one is no longer the protagonist and one becomes the antagonist. Campbell and American post-Jungians in general were not always great about attributing influences and credit where it was due, and Campbell did not do a great job of pointing out that the tradition he was describing was coming from Jung's psychology. Jung was suspicious of the new age theosophists and psychedelic psychonauts that became enamored with the structure of the unconscious for the unconscious sake during the new age movement and the interest in psychedelic drugs and mystical experimentation. Where lament shines
然而，坎贝尔的伦理与荣格的相同。如果一个人被困在英雄之旅的轮子上，或者被困在通往无意识的旅程以及随后回归整合意识的过程中，那么他就不再是主角，而变成了反派。坎贝尔和美国后荣格派总体上并不总是善于归功于影响和来源，坎贝尔也没有很好地指出他所描述的传统来自荣格的心理学。荣格对新时代神智学家和迷幻心理探索者持怀疑态度，这些人在新时代运动和对迷幻药物及神秘实验的兴趣中，迷恋于无意识的结构本身。《哀歌》的闪光点

29
00:20:27.000 --> 00:21:16.000
is when Hillman explains the ethics behind Jung's thinking here. Jung lightly implied this ethics, but was, as Hillman points out, probably not entirely conscious of it. One of lament's biggest strengths and weaknesses is that it sees through the misappropriations of Jungian psychology over the last hundred years. Both of the dialogue figures know the man of Jung so well that they do not need to address how he was misperceived by the public, and they also know the limitations of the knowable. This is another lesson that is discussed in lament. Can modern psychology know what it can't know? That is my biggest complaint with the profession as it currently exists. Modern psychology seems content to retreat into research and objectivism. The medical, corporate, and credentialist academic structure
在于希尔曼解释了荣格在此处的思想背后的伦理。荣格轻描淡写地暗示了这种伦理，但正如希尔曼所指出的，他可能并没有完全意识到这一点。《哀歌》最大的优点和缺点之一是，它看穿了过去一百年来对荣格心理学的挪用。对话中的两位人物对荣格本人非常了解，以至于他们不需要讨论他如何被公众误解，他们也知道可知事物的局限性。这是《哀歌》中讨论的另一个教训。现代心理学能知道它所不能知道的东西吗？这是我目前对这个职业最大的抱怨。现代心理学似乎满足于退回到研究和客观主义中。20世纪80年代心理学的医疗、企业和证书学术结构

30
00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:57.000
of psychology in the 1980s certainly furthered that problem. Jung did not believe that the descent into the unconscious without any hope of return was a path forward for psychology. This is why he abandoned the path that the Red Book was leading him down. Can psychology let go of the objective and the researchable enough to embrace the limits of the knowable? Can we come to terms with limitation enough to heal an ego-inflated world that sees no limits to growth? I don't know, but I sincerely hope so. I said that I would provide a tangible example of the application of this book and its review, so here it is. I have always been enamored with James Hillman.
无疑加剧了这个问题。荣格并不认为没有回归希望的无意识下降是心理学的前进道路。这就是为什么他放弃了《红书》引领他的道路。心理学能否放下客观和可研究的东西，去拥抱可知事物的局限性？我们能否与局限性达成和解，从而治愈一个看不到增长极限、自我膨胀的世界？我不知道，但我衷心希望如此。我说过我会提供一个应用这本书及其书评的具体例子，所以这就是了。我一直很迷恋詹姆斯·希尔曼。

31
00:21:57.000 --> 00:22:40.000
He was by all accounts a brilliant analyst. He was an incredibly intelligent person. And that intellect did not save him. Hillman ended his career as kind of a crank and a failure in my mind. In this book, you see Hillman contemplate that failure. You also see Hillman attempt to redeem himself as he glimpses the unglimpsable. He sees something in the Red Book that he allows to clarify his early attempt to revision psychology. Hellman's attempt to reinvent Jungian psychology as archetypal psychology was wildly derided, largely because it never found any language or technique for application and practice. Hellman himself admitted that he did not know how to practice archetypal psychology.
据各方评价，他是一位出色的分析师。他是一个极其聪明的人。而那种智慧并没有拯救他。在我看来，希尔曼以一种怪人和失败者的身份结束了他的职业生涯。在这本书中，你看到希尔曼思考那种失败。你还看到希尔曼试图通过窥见不可窥见之物来救赎自己。他在《红书》中看到了一些东西，他允许这些东西来澄清他早期重构心理学的尝试。希尔曼试图将荣格心理学重塑为原型心理学的尝试遭到了猛烈的嘲笑，主要是因为它从未找到任何应用和实践的语言或技术。希尔曼本人承认他不知道如何实践原型心理学。

32
00:22:40.000 --> 00:23:27.000
It's easy to laugh at somebody who claims that they have reinvented psychology and they can't even tell you what to do with their revolutionary invention. However, I will admit that I think Hillman was right. He knew that he was right, but he did not know how he was right. It is a mark of arrogance to see yourself as correct without evidence, and Hillman was often arrogant, but I think here he was not. Many Jungian analysts would leave the Jungian institutes through the 70s, 80s, and 90s to start somatic and experiential psychology that used Jung as a map, but the connection between the body and the brain as a technique for direct experience. These models made room for a direct experience in psychology that Jungian analysis does not often do.
嘲笑一个声称自己重塑了心理学却甚至不能告诉你如何使用其革命性发明的人是很容易的。然而，我承认我认为希尔曼是对的。他知道他是对的，但他不知道他是如何对的。在没有证据的情况下认为自己正确是一种傲慢的表现，希尔曼经常很傲慢，但我认为在这里他不是。许多荣格分析师在70、80和90年代离开荣格研究所，开始使用荣格作为地图的躯体和体验心理学，但将身体和大脑之间的联系作为直接体验的技术。这些模型为心理学中的直接体验留出了空间，而荣格分析通常不会这样做。

33
00:23:27.000 --> 00:24:12.000
And it added an element that Jung himself had practiced when he wrote the Red Book. Hillman never found this technique, but he was correct about the path that he saw forward for psychology. He knew what was missing. I started Taproot Therapy Collective because I felt a calling to dig up the Jungian techniques of my parents' generation and reify them. And I also saw those as the most viable map towards the future of psychology, even though American psychology had largely forgotten those techniques. I also saw them devoid of practical technique or application for a world where years of analysis cost more than most trauma patients will make in a lifetime. I feel that the experiential and brain-based medicine techniques like brain spotting are the future of the profession.
它增加了一个荣格本人在撰写《红书》时实践过的元素。希尔曼从未找到这种技术，但他对他所看到的心理学前进道路是正确的。他知道缺少什么。我创办“主根疗法集体”是因为我感到一种召唤，去挖掘我父母那一代的荣格技术并将其具体化。我也认为这些是通往心理学未来最可行的地图，尽管美国心理学在很大程度上已经忘记了这些技术。我也看到它们缺乏针对一个分析费用比大多数创伤患者一生收入还高的世界的实用技术或应用。我觉得像“大脑定位”（Brainspotting）这样的体验式和基于大脑的医学技术是该行业的未来。

34
00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:57.000
Pathways like brain spotting, sensory motor therapy, somatic experiencing, neurostimulation, ketamine, psilocybin, or any other technique that allows for direct experience of the subcortical brain is the path forward to treat trauma. These things will be at odds with the medicalized, corporate, and credentialized nature of healthcare. I knew that this would be poorly understood as a path that few people, even the well-intentioned, would be able to see, but I never would have found it if I had refused the call of the dead that is being talked about in this book. Lament is relevant because none of those realizations is somewhere that I ever would have gotten without the tradition that I am standing on top of. I am, as Isaac Newton said,
像大脑定位、感觉运动疗法、躯体体验、神经刺激、氯胺酮、裸盖菇素或任何其他允许直接体验皮层下大脑的技术，都是治疗创伤的前进道路。这些东西将与医疗化、企业化和证书化的医疗保健性质相冲突。我知道这会被误解为一条很少有人（即使是善意的人）能看到的道路，但如果我拒绝了这本书中所谈论的死者的召唤，我就永远不会找到它。《哀歌》之所以相关，是因为如果没有我所立足的传统，我永远不会得到这些领悟。正如艾萨克·牛顿所说，我

35
00:24:57.000 --> 00:25:49.000
standing on the shoulders of giants, except Isaac Newton didn't invent that phrase. It was associated with him, but he was standing on the tradition of the dead to utter a phrase that was first recorded in the medieval period. We don't remember the person who first said that. The author of its origin is unknown because they are, well, dead, and they have no one to give their eulogy. The ethics and cosmology of lament is that our lives are meant to be a eulogy for our dead. Lament makes every honest eulogy in history become an ethics, and by extension, a cosmology. Read Pericles' eulogy from the Peloponnesian War in Thucydides. How many of these lessons are still unlearned? I would feel disingenuous in my career unless I tell you who these giants are that I stand on.
站在巨人的肩膀上，尽管艾萨克·牛顿并没有发明这个短语。它与他有关，但他站在死者的传统之上，说出了一个最早记录在中世纪的短语。我们不记得第一个说出这句话的人。它的起源作者不详，因为他们，嗯，死了，没有人为他们写悼词。《哀歌》的伦理和宇宙论是，我们的生活注定要成为我们死者的悼词。《哀歌》使历史上每一篇诚实的悼词都成为一种伦理，进而成为一种宇宙论。读读修昔底德《伯罗奔尼撒战争史》中伯里克利的悼词。有多少教训至今仍未被吸取？除非我告诉你我所站立的这些巨人是谁，否则我在我的职业生涯中会感到虚伪。

36
00:25:49.000 --> 00:26:36.000
They are David Tacey, they are John Beebe, they are Carl Jung, they are Sanu Shandasani, they are Fritz Perls, they are Karen Horney, they are Hal Stone, and many, many others. They are all of the people that I use to have the perspective that I have now that I never could have built on my own. I would never have heard the voices of James Hillman inside myself unless I had learned to listen to the dead from his voice beyond the grave, and it would have been easy for me to merely criticize his failures instead of seeing him as incomplete truths. Hillman died with many things incomplete, as we all inevitably will, and Lament helped me clarify the voices that I was hearing in the profession. Lament of the dead is a fascinating read, not because it tells us exactly what to do with the dead or even what they are.
他们是大卫·泰西、约翰·比比（John Beebe）、卡尔·荣格、索努·沙姆达萨尼、弗里茨·皮尔斯（Fritz Perls）、卡伦·霍妮（Karen Horney）、哈尔·斯通（Hal Stone）以及许多许多其他人。他们都是我用来获得我现在所拥有的视角的人，而这些视角我永远无法独自建立。除非我学会了从他超越坟墓的声音中倾听死者，否则我永远不会听到詹姆斯·希尔曼在我内心的声音，而我很容易仅仅批评他的失败，而不是将他视为不完整的真理。希尔曼带着许多未完成的事情去世了，正如我们所有人最终都会那样，而《哀歌》帮助我理清了我在这个行业中听到的声音。《死者的哀歌》是一本引人入胜的读物，不是因为它告诉我们确切地该如何对待死者，甚至不是告诉我们他们是什么。

37
00:26:36.000 --> 00:27:27.000
Lament is fascinating because it helps us to see a mindful path forward between innovation and tradition. The contents of the collective unconscious cannot be contained by one individual. Just as Jungian psychology is meant to be a container to help an individual integrate the forces of the collective unconscious, attention to the unlived life of the historical dead can be a kind of container for our culture. Similarly to Jungian psychology, the container is not meant to be literalized or turned into a prison. It is a lens and a buffer to protect us until we are ready and allow us to see ourselves more clearly once we are. Our project is to go further into the journey of knowing ourselves where our ancestors failed to. Our mindful life is the product of the unlived life
《哀歌》之所以引人入胜，是因为它帮助我们在创新与传统之间看到了一条理性的前进道路。集体无意识的内容不能被一个人所容纳。正如荣格心理学旨在成为一个容器，帮助个人整合集体无意识的力量一样，关注历史死者未竟的生活也可以成为我们文化的一种容器。与荣格心理学类似，这个容器不应被字面化或变成监狱。它是一个透镜和缓冲器，保护我们直到我们准备好，并允许我们在准备好后更清晰地看到自己。我们的项目是深入到我们祖先失败的地方，去探索认识我们自己的旅程。我们理性的生活是死者

